nodecode-cloud-files is a rewrite of electricsheephq's electricsheephq/hermes-cloud-file-manager:

  https://github.com/electricsheephq/hermes-cloud-file-manager
  commit 9db37a6575852cc7bd4aa23b212ec6a52b915ac5
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Electric Sheep (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's file API as five model-facing tools -- files_list,
files_search, files_read, files_save and files_mkdir -- with the folder model
(roots, their ids and labels, absolute folders only), the path guard that
refuses an absolute path, a parent traversal, a symbolic link out of the root
and the folder Nodecode keeps its own config in, resolved segment by segment
before anything is touched, the listing with directories first and its sort by
name or mtime, the name search with * and ? globs, its skipped heavy folders
and its visit cap, the whole-file read that answers the sha256 of the bytes
read, the conflict-checked save that refuses to overwrite a file edited since
it was read and writes through a staging name and a rename, and folder
creation that answers which folders it made. The Python (FastAPI over the
gateway) is Common Lisp here: the filesystem itself, one section declaration
for the settings, and five nlk:define-contract declarations for the tools'
arguments, so the schema the model is shown and the body that runs are one
shape. The "/files" command puts the same folders in front of a person.

Left out: the plugin's browser -- the Hermes Desktop tab with its tree, drag
and drop, chunked upload of whole folders, image and PDF preview, the chat
drop target, folder notes and the Google Drive import. Those are surfaces of
Hermes Desktop and of the agent's own Google skill, and a cell has a shell
and a tool call rather than a window; what a person browses here, they browse
by asking, or with /files. Also left out: the chunked upload protocol
(4 MiB parts, resume and abort) and the 24-hour sweep of its staging
names -- the cell's own writes are whole files, staged as .cfm-<hex>.part
and renamed into place -- and the download of a file as bytes, which a
person does with the tools they already have.
